# Footgun: Causal Message Ordering — CLEAR
#
# CLEAR's concurrency model is based on structured fibers and @shared:locked
# state. It does not have explicit message-passing channels, so the relay
# pattern from C/Go/Rust maps differently.
#
# CLEAR's happens-before guarantees:
#   - NEXT p awaits the BG fiber that produced p. All writes inside that
#     fiber are visible after NEXT returns. This is a full barrier.
#   - WITH / WITH EXCLUSIVE acquire the mutex before any access inside
#     the block. The Mutex acquire is itself a full barrier.
#
# Because BG fibers communicate through @shared:locked values (not raw
# shared memory + a signal), and because WITH blocks are the only access
# path, every read is guarded by a mutex acquire — which subsumes the
# happens-before guarantee. The broken relay pattern from C cannot be
# expressed: there is no way to read shared state without acquiring the
# lock that serializes it with the writer's release.
#
# The causal ordering "footgun" in CLEAR looks different: it is possible
# to observe stale values if you read the same @shared:locked value twice
# from two separate WITH blocks, with another fiber modifying it in between.
# This is the TOCTOU pattern (see 05_toctou), not a memory ordering issue.

STRUCT Payload {
  data: String
}

FN main() RETURNS Void ->
  result = Payload{ data: "" } @shared:locked;

  # Producer: writes data under the lock.
  producer:~ = BG {
    WITH EXCLUSIVE result AS r {
      r.data = "important result";
      # Mutex release here is a full barrier on all architectures.
    }
  };

  # Relay: waits for producer, then signals consumer.
  # NEXT producer ensures all of producer's writes are visible before
  # this fiber proceeds. The happens-before chain is preserved because
  # NEXT is a synchronization point, not a plain channel forward.
  relay:~ = BG {
    NEXT producer;
    # Nothing to forward: the data lives in 'result'. Consumer
    # will see it because WITH EXCLUSIVE issues a mutex acquire.
  };

  # Consumer: waits for relay, then reads data.
  consumer:~ = BG {
    NEXT relay;
    WITH result AS r {
      # Mutex acquire here is a full barrier. Guaranteed to see
      # the producer's write regardless of hardware memory model.
      print("consumer saw: " $+ r.data);
    }
  };

  NEXT consumer;
END

# Summary:
#
# CLEAR sidesteps the causal ordering footgun by eliminating the pattern
# that causes it: shared data is always accessed through a mutex-guarded
# WITH block, and fiber synchronization is always through NEXT (a full
# barrier). There is no way to forward a "signal" separately from the
# data without also forwarding the happens-before.
#
# The footgun still exists at the logical level (TOCTOU across two WITH
# blocks) but not at the memory ordering level (mutex always SC).
